These are brief instructions for installing IMSIL 2025-04 on a Linux system. 
Additional instructions are found in the README file.

1. If you are going to use gfortran, make sure gfortran is installed and
   callable through the command `gfortran`, as well as OpenMP if you are
   going to use the parallel version.
   
   If you are going to use the Intel Fortran compiler, you have to source the
   `setvars.sh` script before calling make. This will define the `ifort`
   command and set some paths needed for compilation and execution.
   
2. In the `dirs.mk` file of the `code` directory, adjust the paths of SRCDIR
   (= path to the `code` directory) and `EXEDIR` (= where you want to have
   the executable). Make sure, the `EXEDIR` ends with `.../code`.
   
3. Go to the `code` directory (SRCDIR) and run `make` (to use the gfortran 
   compiler) or `make FC=ifort` (to use the Intel Fortran compiler).
   
4. In the `imsil.py` script of the `scripts` directory, set `installdir` to
   `EXEDIR` without `/code`.
   
5. In the `makefile` of the `tests` directory, adapt the lines defining IMSIL 
   and MIMSIL (close to the top of the file) by

      IMSIL=python <path-to-imsil.py>
      MIMSIL=python <path-to-mimsil.py>

   Replace "python" by the name of your Python interpreter, if it is called
   differently. Note that you must use a Python 3 interpreter.

6. Test your installation by going to the tests directory and running

      make rrobin
      ./compare rrobin
      
   These are short tests on transmission and backscattering from a thin
   membrane. If successful, run all tests with
   
      make all
      ./compare all
   
   You can also say `make -j <n> all` or `nohup make -j <n> all &` to have
   <n> tests run in parallel (replace <n> with the number of processors you 
   want to use). Another option is to say `make t=<n> all` or 
   `nohup make t=<n> all &` to use the parallel version with <n> threads.
   Note that if you test several of these options, you must touch the .exe
   file in EXEDIR (or all .inp and .tem files in the subdirectories) due to 
   the way make works.
